<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit-Amukani</h1><p class="st-meta">880 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.) · capital Sapia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chaldean tribal state of central Babylonia whose leader Nabu-mukin-zeri seized the throne of Babylon in 732 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bit-Amukani, 'House of Amukanu', was one of the three main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> that settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in the early first millennium BCE. It held land along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> in central and southern Babylonia, around the town of Sapia (Shapiya). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldeans</a> first appear in Assyrian annals in the 9th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-tribes">Chaldean tribes</a> were led by sheikhs who controlled walled towns, date groves and trade routes to the Gulf, and they adopted Babylonian names and culture while keeping tribal organisation. In 732 BCE, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonassar">Nabonassar's</a> son had been murdered by a usurper, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-zeri">Nabu-mukin-zeri of Bit-Amukani</a> seized the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dynasty-of-e">Dynasty of E</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tiglath-Pileser III of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> besieged Sapia in 731 BCE and by 729 BCE had overthrown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-mukin-zeri">Nabu-mukin-zeri</a> and taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. Bit-Amukani later joined revolts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>, including that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamash-shum-ukin">Shamash-shum-ukin</a>, and by the mid-7th century it vanished into Babylonian society.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 880 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:42.19%;width:4.58%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>880 BCE – 650 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sapia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nabu-mukin-zeri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Babylonian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Akkadian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Iraq</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bit Amukkani</dd></dl></aside></div>

